#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:3
msgid "How pipx works"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:7
msgid ""
"This page describes the **pip backend** model, where a shared ``pip`` "
"environment backs every managed venv through a ``.pth`` file. When the **uv "
"backend** is active (the default whenever uv is available, via the "
"``pipx[uv]`` extra or on ``PATH``) pipx skips the shared environment and "
"uses ``uv venv`` and ``uv pip`` instead; uv-created venvs ship without pip. "
"See :doc:`../how-to/use-uv-backend`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:14
msgid "Installing an application"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:16
msgid ""
"``pipx install PACKAGE`` creates an isolated virtual environment under "
"``PIPX_HOME`` (for example ``~/.local/share/pipx/venvs/black``) and installs "
"the package into it. Under the pip backend pipx first creates or reuses a "
"shared environment that holds an up-to-date ``pip`` and exposes it to each "
"managed venv through a `.pth file <https://docs.python.org/3/library/site."
"html>`_, so every venv borrows one pip rather than installing its own. Under "
"the uv backend there is no shared environment: ``uv venv`` builds the venv "
"(without pip) and ``uv pip`` installs into it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:23
msgid ""
"Once the package is installed, pipx exposes the application's resources so "
"you can reach them from anywhere. It handles four kinds:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:26
msgid ""
"console and GUI scripts, into ``PIPX_BIN_DIR`` (default ``~/.local/bin``), "
"for example ``~/.local/bin/black`` -> ``~/.local/share/pipx/venvs/black/bin/"
"black``;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:28
msgid ""
"manual pages, into ``PIPX_MAN_DIR`` (default ``~/.local/share/man/"
"man[1-9]``);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:29
msgid ""
"shell completion scripts, into ``PIPX_COMPLETION_DIR`` (default ``~/.local/"
"share``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:31
msgid ""
"pipx symlinks each resource into place. On Windows, and on any filesystem "
"that does not support symlinks, it copies the file instead. As long as "
"``PIPX_BIN_DIR`` is on your ``PATH`` the commands are available globally, "
"and on systems with ``man`` support the pages are too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:35
msgid ""
"pipx records what it installed and exposed in a per-venv ``pipx_metadata."
"json`` file. Later commands (``upgrade``, ``reinstall``, ``expose``) read it "
"to reproduce the same state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:38
msgid ""
"Adding ``--global`` to any command targets a system-wide location shared by "
"all users instead of your home directory; the concrete paths and overrides "
"live in :doc:`../how-to/configure-paths`. It is not available on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:41
msgid ""
"For a ``.py`` script carrying PEP 723 metadata, pipx builds a temporary "
"wheel from the script and its declared dependencies, installs that wheel "
"like any other package, then records the original path or URL so ``upgrade`` "
"and ``reinstall`` can rebuild it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:69
msgid "Running an application"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:71
msgid ""
"``pipx run APP`` executes an application without installing it permanently. "
"pipx either reuses a cached temporary venv or builds a fresh one, then "
"invokes the app. The cache key is a hash of the package name, spec, Python "
"version, and pip arguments; cached environments expire after 14 days, after "
"which the next run rebuilds against the latest release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:75
msgid ""
"Under the pip backend the temporary venv borrows the shared pip; under the "
"uv backend uv creates and populates it. ``pipx run --with PKG`` adds extra "
"dependencies to that temporary environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:102
msgid "Exposing apps on PATH"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:104
msgid ""
"When you type an exposed command, the shell finds the symlink (or copy) in "
"``PIPX_BIN_DIR`` and follows it to the launcher inside the app's venv. That "
"launcher runs the venv's own Python against the installed package, so the "
"app always uses its isolated dependencies and never your system site-"
"packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:124
msgid "Directory layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:126
msgid ""
"pipx keeps its state under one home directory. The tree below is the "
"conceptual shape; the concrete locations, defaults, and every override live "
"in :doc:`../how-to/configure-paths`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:153
msgid ""
"You can do all of this yourself; pipx automates it. Pass ``--verbose`` to "
"see each command it runs and to stream installer output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:158
msgid "Resolving the Python interpreter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:160
msgid ""
"pipx prefers a real system Python and only downloads a standalone build as a "
"fallback. When you pass ``--python``, it tries the value as a literal path, "
"then as a command on ``PATH``, then as a ``pythonX.Y`` command, then through "
"the ``py`` launcher on Windows. Only if every step fails, and ``--fetch-"
"python`` permits it, does pipx fetch a build from `python-build-standalone "
"<https://github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone>`_. The default, ``--"
"fetch-python=never``, keeps pipx offline and errors out instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/how-pipx-works.rst:166
msgid ""
"For the authoritative step-by-step order, the ``--fetch-python`` values, and "
"when a downloaded build beats a patched system Python, see :doc:`../how-to/"
"standalone-python`."
msgstr ""
